59-      <section id="what-happened">
60-        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
61-        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">On October 2, 2026, G7 leaders agreed in their statement to a coordinated release of 100 million barrels through the International Energy Agency "to begin immediately over 4 months", "taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled", with what they called a "frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days by G7 members and partners". The same statement says the group will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns and "temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible", and "We also encourage engagement with countries holding significant refining capacities to boost global production of refined products, particularly diesel". It reaffirms a commitment to refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments with a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="temporarily increase utilization rates where feasible" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We also encourage engagement with countries holding significant refining capacities to boost global production of refined products, particularly diesel" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries" /></p>
62:        <p data-layer="narrative">The French presidency's readout states a ceiling, "jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois", up to 100 million barrels within 4 months. That readout names refined products, notably diesel and crude.<Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="notamment de diesel et de pétrole brut, jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /></p>
63-        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">In March, International Energy Agency members pledged 400 million barrels; by October 2 around 325 million had been released, and that day the G7 set 100 million barrels over 4 months without saying how much of it is beyond the March pledge. The October 2 statement asks the agency to "monitor the immediate and full implementation of the March 2026 commitments".<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="coordinated release of 400 million barrels" /><Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="He noted that around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released, representing over 80% of the 400 million barrels originally pledged in the action." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="monitor the immediate and full implementation of the March 2026 commitments" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments with a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /></p>
64-        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 30 President Donald Trump said he was still "thinking about" banning diesel exports. On October 2, hours after the G7 agreement, he said he will not authorize an export ban on diesel, and the joint statement commits the seven countries to refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between themselves.<Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage={`still "thinking about" banning diesel exports`} /><Cite s="reuters-no-export-ban-2026-10-02" passage="he will not authorize an export ban on diesel" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries" /></p>
65-        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">The IEA's March contributions page says the overall release of emergency stocks will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products. A barrel counted as crude is not the diesel a pump sells. On September 30, Energy Secretary Chris Wright said diesel supplies are "tight" because of export loss related to conflicts in Russia and the Middle East.<Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="the overall release of emergency stocks will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products" /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage={`diesel supplies are "tight" because of export loss related to conflicts in Russia and the Middle East`} /></p>
66:        <p data-layer="narrative">The October 2 statement calls for the immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz. Net exports of diesel/gasoil from the Gulf countries averaged 390,000 barrels a day in August, just over a quarter of levels before the war in the Middle East. The French presidency's October 2 readout says volumes moving through Hormuz and the Yanbu canal in the Red Sea have been increasing in recent days.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="averaged 390 kb/d in August, just over a quarter of pre-war levels" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="Combined, net exports of diesel/gasoil exports from the Gulf and Russia in August were 1.6 mb/d lower than in February" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="Les volumes acheminés transitant par Ormuz et dans le canal de Yanbu en Mer Rouge augmentent ces derniers jours" /></p>
67-
68-        <SourcedBlock source="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
69-          <p>The same statement says the release takes into account "commitments that have already been fulfilled," asks the International Energy Agency to monitor the March 2026 commitments, and says the group will meet again "to discuss the possibility of additional diesel releases as necessary." It also says the members "reaffirm our commitment to refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries," and that a follow-up report "should be delivered before 20 days."<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="to discuss the possibility of additional diesel releases as necessary" /></p>
70-        </SourcedBlock>
71-        <SourcedBlock source="elysee-g7-statement-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
72:          <p>The French presidency published the same English statement, including the {release.barrels} barrel release over {release.months} and the diesel release "within the first 20 days."<Cite s="elysee-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /></p>
73-        </SourcedBlock>
74-        <SourcedBlock source="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" kind="record" detail>
75-          <p>On March 11, Chris Wright said 32 member nations agreed to a "coordinated release of 400 million barrels," and that the United States would "release 172 million barrels from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, beginning next week," over "approximately 120 days." He said the United States had arranged to replace the draw with "approximately 200 million barrels within the next year."<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="release 172 million barrels from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, beginning next week" /><Cite s="cnbc-export-ban-2026-09-30" passage="American refiners are running at record highs" /></p>
76-        </SourcedBlock>
--
86-          <p>It says the previous record high was 199.09p, set in June 2022.<Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="The previous record high was 199.09p, set in June 2022" /></p>
87-          <p>The same article says prices have rocketed because of the US-Israeli conflict with Iran and Russia's ongoing war in Ukraine.<Cite s="independent-miliband-2026-10-02" passage="Prices have rocketed in recent months because of the disruption in global markets caused by the US-Israeli conflict with Iran and Russia’s ongoing war in Ukraine." /></p>
88-        </SourcedBlock>
89-        <SourcedBlock source="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" kind="record" detail>
90:          <p>The French presidency's readout says the G7 leaders decided unanimously. It says a letter from the president of the Republic to the president of the European Commission concerned running refineries at full capacity, and that first measures had already been decided at European level.<Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="Ils ont décidé, à l'unanimité :" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="la lettre que le Président de la République avait envoyée à la Présidente de la Commission européenne" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="Les volumes acheminés transitant par Ormuz et dans le canal de Yanbu en Mer Rouge augmentent ces derniers jours" /></p>
91-        </SourcedBlock>
92-        <SourcedBlock source="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" kind="record" detail>
93-          <p>The International Energy Agency's March 19 contributions page describes the 11 March agreement as one "to make 400 million barrels of oil available to the market". It says the overall release "will largely consist of crude oil, while in Europe the contributions will primarily take the form of refined oil products." It calls the action "the sixth time" members have acted together since the agency "was created in 1974," after actions "in 1991, 2005, 2011, and twice in 2022," and calls the Middle East war "the largest supply disruption in the history of the global oil market." Executive Director Fatih Birol's remarks are named on that page.<Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="to make 400 million barrels of oil available to the market" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="This is the sixth time that IEA Member countries have taken emergency collective action" /><Cite s="iea-contributions-2026-03-19" passage="Previous collective actions were taken in 1991, 2005, 2011, and twice in 2022." /><Cite s="iea-contributions-workbook-2026-03-19" passage="Total IEA,426.03075080054771" /></p>
94-        </SourcedBlock>

